%[text] # Lab 1: Detect and Count Cell Nuclei Using MATLAB Apps
%[text] Hands-on workshop | MATLAB R2026b | about 40 minutes
%[text] This lab follows the first part of the MathWorks example *Detect and Count Cell Nuclei in Whole Slide Images*, but every step is done in an app instead of code. You will use two apps:
%[text] - **Color Thresholder**: detect nuclei by their colour (section *Detect Nuclei Using Color Thresholding*).
%[text] - **Image Region Analyzer**: keep only objects with the size of a single nucleus (section *Select Nuclei Using Image Region Properties*). \
%[text] Work from top to bottom. Click **Run Section** only on sections that contain code; the other steps are clicks in the apps. Set MATLAB's **Current Folder** to the folder that contains this file and the `data` folder.
%[text] Required products: MATLAB and Image Processing Toolbox.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Lab steps"}
%%
%[text] ## Step 0: Where does this image come from?
%[text] Pathologists scan tissue slides into **whole slide images (WSI)**. The slide used here is a lymph node stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) from the Camelyon17 data set. At full resolution it is 197,226 × 96,651 pixels (about 57 GB in memory), so it cannot be opened like a normal photo. Instead we develop the method on a small **region of interest (ROI)** of 824 × 660 pixels, cut from the full-resolution level exactly as in the MathWorks example. One pixel is about 0.243 µm.
%[text] ![Whole slide (left) and the region of interest used in this lab (right)](text:image:l1a0)
%[text] In H&E staining, **hematoxylin** colours cell nuclei dark purple-blue, and **eosin** colours cytoplasm and connective tissue pink. Our goal: find the dark purple nuclei and count them.
I = imread(fullfile("data","lymph_node_roi.png"));
imshow(I) %[output:0bd2bc67]
title("Region of interest: lymph node, H&E") %[output:0bd2bc67]
%%
%[text] # Part A: Detect Nuclei Using Color Thresholding
%[text] ## Step 1: Open Color Thresholder with the image
%[text] Run this section. It opens the **Color Thresholder** app with the ROI already loaded.
%[text] *Alternative without code:* **APPS** tab › **Image Processing and Computer Vision** › **Color Thresholder**, then **Load Image** › **Load Image From Workspace** (variable `I`) or **Load Image From File** (`data/lymph_node_roi.png`).
colorThresholder(I)
%[text] ![Load Image menu in Color Thresholder](text:image:l1a1)
%%
%[text] ## Step 2: Choose a color space
%[text] The app first shows the image in four **color spaces**. Each 3-D plot shows every pixel as a point, positioned by its colour values. Rotate a plot with the mouse to see how well the purple nuclei separate from the pink tissue.
%[text] - **RGB**: red, green, blue amounts (what the camera records).
%[text] - **HSV**: hue (colour type), saturation (colour strength), value (brightness).
%[text] - **YCbCr**: brightness plus two colour-difference channels (used in video).
%[text] - **L\*a\*b\***: lightness plus two colour axes designed to match human perception. \
%[text] Click **RGB** (the MathWorks example uses RGB for nuclei).
%[text] ![b\*](text:image:l1a2)
%%
%[text] ## Step 3: Adjust the R, G and B sliders
%[text] The RGB tab shows the image, one histogram per channel and a 3-D point cloud. Each histogram has two handles: pixels whose value lies **between** the handles are kept.
%[text] ![RGB tab before any adjustment: all pixels are kept](text:image:l1a3)
%[text] Nuclei are **dark** in red and green (purple = little red light and little green light passes through). So:
%[text] 1. Drag the **right handle of R** to the left, to about **140**.
%[text] 2. Drag the **right handle of G** to the left, to about **120**.
%[text] 3. Leave **B** at the full range 0–255. \
%[text] With **Live Update** on, the background turns black as soon as you move a handle. Stop when the nuclei stay purple and most pink tissue disappears. Small differences from these numbers are fine.
%[text] ![Thresholds adjusted in the MathWorks example on this same ROI](text:image:l1a4)
%[text] *Tip:* you can also draw a polygon around a cluster of points in the 3-D point cloud to select colours. **Reset Thresholds** returns to the full range; **Invert Mask** swaps kept and removed pixels.
%%
%[text] ## Step 4: Check the binary mask
%[text] Click **Show Binary**. White = pixels you selected (candidate nuclei), black = everything else. Look for two common problems: nuclei with holes in the middle (paler chromatin), and neighbouring nuclei that touch and merge into one white blob. Part B handles both.
%[text] ![Expected result: masked image and binary mask](text:image:l1a5)
%%
%[text] ## Step 5: Export the mask to the workspace
%[text] Click **Export** › **Export Images**. In the dialog, keep **Binary Mask** checked, name it **`BW`**, and click **OK**.
%[text] ![](text:image:9853)
%[text] ![Export To Workspace dialog: name the binary mask BW](text:image:l1a7)
%[text] *Good to know:* **Export Function** writes a MATLAB function (in the MathWorks example `createNucleiMask.m`) that repeats exactly these thresholds on any new image. This is how a few clicks become code you can run on thousands of image tiles.
%%
%[text] ## Step 6: Check the mask over the image
%[text] Run this section. Green/magenta show where the mask and the image differ, so you can see whether the white areas really sit on the nuclei.
assert(exist("BW","var")==1,"Export the Binary Mask as BW from Color Thresholder first (Step 5).");
imshow(imfuse(I,BW)) %[output:7cc65a6f]
title("Nuclei mask over the image") %[output:7cc65a6f]
fprintf("Mask covers %.0f%% of the image\n",100*mean(BW(:))) %[output:35186014]
%[text] ![Expected overlay](text:image:l1a8)
%%
%[text] # Part B: Select Nuclei Using Image Region Properties
%[text] ## Step 7: Open Image Region Analyzer with the mask
%[text] Run this section. The **Image Region Analyzer** shows the mask on the left and a **Region Properties** table on the right: one row per connected white object (a *region*).
imageRegionAnalyzer(BW)
%[text] ![Image Region Analyzer opened with BW](text:image:l1a9)
%%
%[text] ## Step 8: Fill holes
%[text] In the **ADD/REMOVE** group, check **Fill Holes**. Holes inside a nucleus are filled, so each nucleus becomes one solid region and its area is measured correctly.
%[text] *Exclude Border* would remove every region that touches the image edge. We leave it off here; when the method runs on many tiles, overlapping tile borders take care of edge nuclei instead.
%[text] ![Fill Holes checked](text:image:l1a10)
%%
%[text] ## Step 9: Choose the properties to measure
%[text] Click **Choose Properties** and tick at least **Area**, **Centroid**, **EquivDiameter**, **Perimeter** and **Circularity**.
%[text] - **Area**: number of pixels in the region.
%[text] - **Centroid**: centre position (x, y) of the region.
%[text] - **EquivDiameter**: diameter of a circle with the same area.
%[text] - **Perimeter**: length of the boundary.
%[text] - **Circularity**: 1 for a perfect circle, lower for irregular shapes. \
%[text] ![](text:image:4a03)
%%
%[text] ## Step 10: Find the size of a single nucleus
%[text] Click the **Area** column header to sort the regions. Click a row to highlight that region in the image.
%[text] - At the **top** (largest areas) you find merged blobs of several touching nuclei. Scroll down until the highlighted region is one single nucleus: in the MathWorks example this is **405 pixels** (EquivDiameter about 22.7 pixels).
%[text] - At the **bottom** (smallest areas) you find specks and nuclei cut by the image edge. Scroll up until the region is a whole nucleus: in the MathWorks example this is **229 pixels**. \
%[text] ![Sorting by Area: the largest regions are merged nuclei (MathWorks example, same ROI)](text:image:l1a12)
%[text] **Think:** 229 to 405 pixels is about 13.5 to 24 µm² (0.243 µm per pixel), an equivalent diameter of about 4 to 5.5 µm. Does that fit the size of a lymphocyte nucleus?
%%
%[text] ## Step 11: Filter regions by area
%[text] Click **Filter**. In the **Filter Regions** dialog, check the first row and set **Area** › **Between** › **229** and **405**. Click **Apply**, then **Close**.
%[text] ![Filter Regions: Area between 229 and 405](text:image:l1a13)
%[text] Only single-nucleus-sized regions remain. Merged blobs and specks are gone.
%[text] ![Result after filtering](text:image:l1a14)
%[text] **Trade-off:** merged nuclei are now ignored, so the count is slightly too low. A more advanced method (watershed or a deep learning model such as Cellpose) can split touching nuclei.
%%
%[text] ## Step 12: Export the properties
%[text] Click **Export** › **Export Properties**. Keep **Property table**, name it **`propsTable`**, and click **OK**. (Export Image saves the filtered mask; Export Function writes the filtering steps as a MATLAB function, `calculateNucleiProperties.m` in the MathWorks example.)
%[text] ![](text:image:0f48)
%[text] ![Export To Workspace: property table](text:image:l1a16)
%%
%[text] ## Step 13: Count and show the nuclei
%[text] Run this section. It counts the exported regions, marks each centroid, and converts the areas to µm².
assert(exist("propsTable","var")==1,"Export Properties as propsTable from Image Region Analyzer first (Step 12).");
assert(all(ismember(["Area","Centroid"],propsTable.Properties.VariableNames)), ...
    "Tick Area and Centroid in Choose Properties (Step 9), then export propsTable again.");
nNuclei = height(propsTable)
pixelSize_um = 0.2431;                      % from the slide metadata (1/41136 cm)
areaUm2 = propsTable.Area * pixelSize_um^2;
fprintf("Median nucleus area: %.1f um^2 (%.0f px)\n",median(areaUm2),median(propsTable.Area))
imshow(I); hold on
c = propsTable.Centroid;
plot(c(:,1),c(:,2),"y.",MarkerSize=12)
hold off
title(nNuclei + " nuclei detected (yellow = centroid)")
%[text] ![Expected result: about 265 nuclei with the suggested settings](text:image:l1a17)
%[text] **Record:** your count, your R and G thresholds, and one place where the result looks wrong (missed nucleus, merged nuclei, or tissue counted as a nucleus).
%%
%[text] # Part C: From one region to the whole slide (instructor demo)
%[text] You developed the method on 0.03% of one slide. The MathWorks example scales it up without changing it:
%[text] 1. Open the slide as a **blockedImage** (it reads only the pieces it needs, at any of 9 resolution levels).
%[text] 2. Make a quick **tissue mask** at a coarse level so empty glass is skipped.
%[text] 3. Split the tissue into blocks of 2048 × 2048 pixels with **selectBlockLocations**.
%[text] 4. Run the two exported functions on every block with **apply**, in parallel, with an 11-pixel overlap so nuclei on block borders are counted once.
%[text] 5. Show a **heatmap of nuclei density** next to the slide. \
%[text] The apps you just used produced the core of that pipeline.
%%
%[text] ## Reflection
%[text] - How did the count change when you moved the R or G handle by 10 to 20 levels?
%[text] - Why is the area filter specific to this stain, scanner and magnification?
%[text] - Which regions would you ask a pathologist to check? \
%[text] Reference: [Detect and Count Cell Nuclei in Whole Slide Images (MathWorks)](https://www.mathworks.com/help/images/detect-and-count-cell-nuclei-in-wsi-using-blocked-image.html). Data: Camelyon17 challenge, patient\_000\_node\_1 (https://camelyon17.grand-challenge.org). Screenshots in steps 3 (adjusted sliders) and 10 are from the MathWorks example on the same ROI; generic menus and dialogs (steps 1, 5, 9, 12) were captured on another H&E image; all other screenshots were captured on this lab's data in MATLAB R2026b.

